  urldate = {2022-09-20},
  abstract = {In probability theory, Donsker's theorem (also known as Donsker's invariance principle, or the functional central limit theorem), named after Monroe D. Donsker, is a functional extension of the central limit theorem.  Let                                    X                        1                             ,                    X                        2                             ,                    X                        3                             ,         \ldots{}                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle X\_\{1\},X\_\{2\},X\_\{3\},\textbackslash ldots \}    be a sequence of independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables with mean 0 and variance 1. Let                                    S                        n                             :=                    {$\sum$}                        i             =             1                                   n                                        X                        i                                     \{\textbackslash displaystyle S\_\{n\}:=\textbackslash sum \_\{i=1\}\^\{n\}X\_\{i\}\}   . The stochastic process                         S         :=         (                    S                        n                                        )                        n             {$\in$}                            N                                                  \{\textbackslash displaystyle S:=(S\_\{n\})\_\{n\textbackslash in \textbackslash mathbb \{N\} \}\}    is known as a random walk. Define the diffusively rescaled random walk (partial-sum process) by                                   W                        (             n             )                             (         t         )         :=                                                S                                {$\lfloor$}                 n                 t                 {$\rfloor$}                                                        n                                          ,                  t         {$\in$}         [         0         ,         1         ]         .                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle W\^\{(n)\}(t):=\{\textbackslash frac \{S\_\{\textbackslash lfloor nt\textbackslash rfloor \}\}\{\textbackslash sqrt \{n\}\}\},\textbackslash qquad t\textbackslash in [0,1].\}   The central limit theorem asserts that                                    W                        (             n             )                             (         1         )                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle W\^\{(n)\}(1)\}    converges in distribution to a standard Gaussian random variable                         W         (         1         )                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle W(1)\}    as                         n         \textrightarrow{}         {$\infty$}                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle n\textbackslash to \textbackslash infty \}   . Donsker's invariance principle extends this convergence to the whole function                                    W                        (             n             )                             :=         (                    W                        (             n             )                             (         t         )                    )                        t             {$\in$}             [             0             ,             1             ]                                     \{\textbackslash displaystyle W\^\{(n)\}:=(W\^\{(n)\}(t))\_\{t\textbackslash in [0,1]\}\}   . More precisely, in its modern form, Donsker's invariance principle states that: As random variables taking values in the Skorokhod space                                                 D                             [         0         ,         1         ]                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle \{\textbackslash mathcal \{D\}\}[0,1]\}   , the random function                                    W                        (             n             )                                     \{\textbackslash displaystyle W\^\{(n)\}\}    converges in distribution to a standard Brownian motion                         W         :=         (         W         (         t         )                    )                        t             {$\in$}             [             0             ,             1             ]                                     \{\textbackslash displaystyle W:=(W(t))\_\{t\textbackslash in [0,1]\}\}    as                         n         \textrightarrow{}         {$\infty$}         .                 \{\textbackslash displaystyle n\textbackslash to \textbackslash infty .\}},
